WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:03.669
Let's go ahead and continue our conversation

2
00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:07.419
talking about basic lighting and shadow concepts.

3
00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:09.490
Let's go ahead and dive right in.

4
00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.750
So one of the things I'm gonna continually point out

5
00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:13.490
to you is some of the Lumen settings

6
00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.330
you have to keep in mind when working

7
00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.210
with the post-process volume.

8
00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:20.809
You'll see that there is a Lumen scene lighting quality

9
00:00:20.809 --> 00:00:23.170
as well as a Lumen scene detail.

10
00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:25.769
Now Lumen scene lighting quality,

11
00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:28.289
this is gonna allow us to be able to calculate

12
00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:31.489
with higher fidelity our visuals and our scene.

13
00:00:31.489 --> 00:00:34.289
Now keep in mind as you go higher

14
00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:36.130
this can be GPU expensive.

15
00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.210
And you'll see me point that out many times

16
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.979
in the breakdowns in the slide deck.

17
00:00:40.979 --> 00:00:43.759
So you'll also see a Lumen scene detail.

18
00:00:43.759 --> 00:00:46.280
This controls the size of instances

19
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.179
that can be calculated in a scene.

20
00:00:48.179 --> 00:00:50.359
So you'll see if you have larger settings

21
00:00:50.359 --> 00:00:52.000
it'll help smaller objects,

22
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.280
but it again, it is GPU expensive.

23
00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.899
There's also a Lumen scene distance.

24
00:00:57.939 --> 00:00:59.500
This sets a maximum view distance

25
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.020
of the scene that Lumen maintains for ray tracing against.

26
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.980
Now, this is nice to be able to dial this down.

27
00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.319
If your scene is small

28
00:01:07.319 --> 00:01:10.719
you may not want everything calculated all at one time

29
00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:14.180
or at least pared down to particular region.

30
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:18.599
Again, you can do that via your post-process volume too

31
00:01:18.599 --> 00:01:21.140
as an encapsulated for a particular region

32
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:22.659
or like we did in the beginning

33
00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:26.560
make it have an infinite build so that it's controlling

34
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.879
or influencing the whole environment.

35
00:01:28.879 --> 00:01:32.239
But this gives you a secondary dial down for that.

36
00:01:32.239 --> 00:01:34.040
The final gather quality is important

37
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.680
because as you scale this up

38
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.200
this can reduce noise in your scene.

39
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.459
And you'll also see their screen traces.

40
00:01:40.459 --> 00:01:42.879
I like to use a C-bar which we go over

41
00:01:42.879 --> 00:01:45.719
in our intro to lighting class

42
00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:50.280
which can prevent screen traces or strange reflections

43
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.099
and refractions from popping up

44
00:01:53.099 --> 00:01:54.819
when you move your camera around.

45
00:01:54.819 --> 00:01:56.700
Now, this tries to help that a little bit

46
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.260
but again, there is a C-bar we go over in that class

47
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.540
that can actually support that a little bit better.

48
00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.180
There's max trace distance

49
00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.540
as well as scene capture, catch resolution.

50
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.500
Now, all these you can crank up if you want to

51
00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.340
but I recommend it goes slow and then go high.

52
00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.539
You'll see the max trace distance here

53
00:02:15.539 --> 00:02:20.539
because not everything is by default right here at 20,000.

54
00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.120
Not everything needs to go higher.

55
00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.900
Like this can stay steady

56
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.960
and you can also lower some things.

57
00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.759
So you'll see Lumen scene lighting quality.

58
00:02:28.759 --> 00:02:30.900
We can keep that at a 1.5.

59
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.620
We don't necessarily have to go higher.

60
00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.419
You have to try it out and test it as you go.

61
00:02:35.419 --> 00:02:37.780
Again, you don't want to crank everything out

62
00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.219
up to the maximum all at one time.

63
00:02:40.219 --> 00:02:43.889
You want to go low, then go high.

64
00:02:43.889 --> 00:02:46.430
You'll also notice that you can influence

65
00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.849
a bit of your visuals

66
00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:50.289
with the ability to be able to control your roughness

67
00:02:50.289 --> 00:02:52.129
with a roughness dial down

68
00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:54.189
that you get within your shader.

69
00:02:54.189 --> 00:02:56.870
But there's also a roughness control

70
00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:01.530
inside of the post-process volume for a sweeping control.

71
00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:05.370
This can help with any noise that may be persistent.

72
00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.990
You'll notice with a high reflective surface

73
00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:12.469
noise gets pretty loud there and it shows up pretty strong

74
00:03:12.469 --> 00:03:15.310
or you can dial up the roughness

75
00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:21.879
and eliminate some of the noise that may occur.

76
00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:24.000
So you'll see Lumen reflections here.

77
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.599
Also kind of the same thing.

78
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.879
You want to start low, then go high.

79
00:03:28.879 --> 00:03:31.599
And you'll see that we have a method that we can choose too.

80
00:03:31.599 --> 00:03:34.680
So you can actually use and pair things up

81
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.439
where you have a Lumen reflectivity

82
00:03:37.439 --> 00:03:39.719
but then you have maybe screen space GI

83
00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.879
and that can also be used in games.

84
00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:44.680
You can pair things together if you need to.

85
00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:47.300
Now, this is the CVAR I told you guys about

86
00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.000
where you can do screen traces

87
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.039
and you can use Lumen reflection screen traces.

88
00:03:52.039 --> 00:03:53.840
And this actually works a bit better

89
00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.400
than working with the screen traces

90
00:03:56.039 --> 00:03:57.680
that we were talking about earlier.

91
00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.080
High quality translucency reflections

92
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.840
activating high quality mirror reflections

93
00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.319
on the front layer on a translucent surface.

94
00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:08.860
But again, when it is higher, it is at a higher cost.

95
00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.479
So you have to keep all these things in mind

96
00:04:10.479 --> 00:04:11.500
when you're working with stuff.

97
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.979
And here's the max roughness trace I told you about.

98
00:04:14.979 --> 00:04:16.300
So be careful with that

99
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:19.100
but that allows you to do a sweeping control.

100
00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:22.740
If your surfaces you're noticing need that.

101
00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.420
Let's go and jump into Unreal.

102
00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.540
And what we're gonna do is start building a few things out

103
00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.540
and later on in other lectures

104
00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:33.019
we'll talk about mega lights.

105
00:04:33.019 --> 00:04:37.379
So right now we're in the interior of the kit bash scene

106
00:04:37.379 --> 00:04:39.819
that I created for you guys to play with.

107
00:04:39.819 --> 00:04:43.779
It's in the same scene that we had before our project

108
00:04:43.779 --> 00:04:45.699
which is our UF.

109
00:04:45.699 --> 00:04:48.300
This is a scene we used for Unreal Fest

110
00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.899
world building concepts and lighting.

111
00:04:50.899 --> 00:04:53.639
Let's go and jump in there and take a look

112
00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:56.439
and see what we can do to make things

113
00:04:56.439 --> 00:04:58.319
even look better with the lighting.

114
00:04:58.319 --> 00:04:59.360
Now it's pretty dark here.

115
00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:00.839
We can see your character.

116
00:05:00.839 --> 00:05:02.800
She's kind of sitting in the floor here.

117
00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:04.620
Let's push her up a little bit.

118
00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:05.800
There you go.

119
00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.240
That's a little more ergonomically correct.

120
00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.360
So what we're gonna do now

121
00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.879
is mess with the lighting here and exposure.

122
00:05:11.879 --> 00:05:15.379
We see that we have our post-process volume.

123
00:05:15.379 --> 00:05:17.600
Now we can decide whether we want this to again

124
00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.879
control the whole environment

125
00:05:19.879 --> 00:05:22.079
or make it so it's infinite extended bound.

126
00:05:22.079 --> 00:05:25.160
In this case, we have infinite extended bound.

127
00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.920
Let's go back to our method of global illumination.

128
00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:30.019
Let's pop this up and turn these on.

129
00:05:30.019 --> 00:05:32.500
So we have ray lighting mode.

130
00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:33.839
We're using the project settings.

131
00:05:33.839 --> 00:05:35.959
And again, this can be used as an override

132
00:05:35.959 --> 00:05:38.560
and we're gonna mess with the lumen lighting quality.

133
00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.560
We'll bump this up just a little bit

134
00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.560
to remove some of the noise that may occur

135
00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.079
as well as the detail.

136
00:05:44.079 --> 00:05:47.639
And final gather is also gonna help fix some of the noise.

137
00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:49.920
Now we're not gonna go all the way up to two

138
00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.360
but we're gonna get kind of close

139
00:05:51.519 --> 00:05:52.560
because there is a little bit of noise

140
00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.279
that occurs in the materials.

141
00:05:54.279 --> 00:05:57.079
But again, we can control that via reflectivity

142
00:05:57.079 --> 00:05:59.360
with the noise slider

143
00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:02.279
or we can do that via the material itself.

144
00:06:02.279 --> 00:06:03.259
There's a screen distance.

145
00:06:03.259 --> 00:06:05.720
I'll turn that on, but we're not necessarily gonna use it

146
00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:08.920
and we don't necessarily need screen traces at this time.

147
00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:12.800
And again, that may help if you run into some problems

148
00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:15.759
with some refraction highlighted areas

149
00:06:15.759 --> 00:06:18.720
that seem to disappear and reappear in your scene.

150
00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.879
And we have the scene capture, catch resolution scale.

151
00:06:21.879 --> 00:06:24.560
And again, scale factor for lumen surface,

152
00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:26.639
catch resolution for scene capture.

153
00:06:26.639 --> 00:06:31.120
Smaller values have GPU, save GPU memory

154
00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:32.939
at a cost and quality.

155
00:06:32.939 --> 00:06:34.879
The default is 0.5.

156
00:06:34.879 --> 00:06:37.199
We're gonna keep ours at one, which is fine.

157
00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:38.759
It says the default is 0.5,

158
00:06:38.759 --> 00:06:42.000
but you'll notice it doesn't have a return to sender

159
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.519
or go back to its original state here.

160
00:06:44.519 --> 00:06:46.660
So it looks like the default is one.

161
00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:48.839
So that's just a little bit of a pro tip

162
00:06:48.839 --> 00:06:50.540
we may need to edit later.

163
00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.420
And we're gonna go in here and go to lumen reflections.

164
00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:58.300
And we have a lot of reflective surfaces in here.

165
00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.819
So I'm gonna not mess with the screen traces here.

166
00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:03.899
Again, this can be a problem.

167
00:07:03.899 --> 00:07:04.819
If you move your camera

168
00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:06.720
and you'll notice a hotspot disappears,

169
00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:07.860
clipping at the end.

170
00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.660
And again, that CVAR I shared with you

171
00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:14.060
is a better CVAR to work with than the actual tick box.

172
00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:17.379
And let's go high quality translucency.

173
00:07:17.379 --> 00:07:18.519
Sure, we'll do that.

174
00:07:18.519 --> 00:07:20.279
We do have some mirrored surfaces

175
00:07:20.279 --> 00:07:23.680
such as the windows here on the side.

176
00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.459
And let's go back to that post-process volume.

177
00:07:28.459 --> 00:07:31.540
And we'll have a max refraction bounces

178
00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:36.509
and we'll set that to like maybe like a five.

179
00:07:36.509 --> 00:07:37.350
There we go.

180
00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:38.550
There comes a point where your eye

181
00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:40.269
is not going to pick it up.

182
00:07:40.269 --> 00:07:43.709
You can set it at like five or seven.

183
00:07:43.709 --> 00:07:45.110
They just won't be able to pick it up.

184
00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.069
We give you the luxury and the option

185
00:07:47.069 --> 00:07:48.589
to be able to increase that,

186
00:07:48.589 --> 00:07:51.069
but you're really not gonna get that.

187
00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:53.509
And your human eye is not gonna really pick up

188
00:07:53.509 --> 00:07:56.129
the finite details from a certain point.

189
00:07:56.129 --> 00:07:58.470
That's kind of like you've reached the limit.

190
00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:00.470
All right, so let's put some lights in here.

191
00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:02.230
And what we're gonna start off with

192
00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:05.110
is gonna use a Rec Light.

193
00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:07.290
This is one of my favorite lights to use.

194
00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:09.550
This is one I used a whole bunch

195
00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:10.750
for Love, Death and Robots

196
00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:14.350
when Unreal was partnering with Blur Studios.

197
00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:18.189
And I'm gonna go ahead and rotate this 90 degrees.

198
00:08:18.189 --> 00:08:19.189
There we go.

199
00:08:19.189 --> 00:08:20.709
And we're gonna make it so it's movable

200
00:08:20.709 --> 00:08:23.250
because you wanna make sure your lights are movable

201
00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:26.230
when you're using Lumen

202
00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:28.810
because that makes it so that updates on the fly

203
00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:32.629
and works best with our virtual shadow maps.

204
00:08:32.629 --> 00:08:34.230
Now, a lot of people's tendency

205
00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:35.250
when we're working with lights,

206
00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:37.690
we'll say, hey, I'm gonna put a light here

207
00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:39.509
and I'm gonna put on each one.

208
00:08:39.509 --> 00:08:40.509
Now, believe it or not,

209
00:08:40.509 --> 00:08:42.629
that can get expensive after a while.

210
00:08:42.629 --> 00:08:44.789
So one of the better ways to handle that

211
00:08:44.789 --> 00:08:47.909
is to go in here and make it so that the light,

212
00:08:47.909 --> 00:08:49.590
especially if they're close together,

213
00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:53.389
you can use a single light such as this Rec Light

214
00:08:53.389 --> 00:08:55.870
and you can stretch it along that length

215
00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:57.590
of how these are set.

216
00:08:57.590 --> 00:09:01.090
So I'm gonna go in here and set my width here

217
00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:06.149
a little bit and we're gonna move it over.

218
00:09:06.149 --> 00:09:09.879
And we want it to encompass all these lights

219
00:09:09.879 --> 00:09:13.480
and we'll make the width a little bit bigger.

220
00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:16.269
There we go.

221
00:09:16.269 --> 00:09:20.149
And we can make it even a little bit bigger than that

222
00:09:20.149 --> 00:09:25.419
so it covers all of them just enough

223
00:09:25.419 --> 00:09:27.460
and move that over right there.

224
00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:28.299
Awesome.

225
00:09:28.299 --> 00:09:29.120
Now look at that.

226
00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:31.100
No one is the wiser with that.

227
00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:32.940
That's a set of lights that we set in

228
00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:34.500
using that Rec Light.

229
00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.299
We can choose from here to do the barn door

230
00:09:37.299 --> 00:09:38.620
if we want to.

231
00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:41.279
Now the barn door angle and the length

232
00:09:41.279 --> 00:09:45.019
as well as mainly our source width and source height

233
00:09:45.019 --> 00:09:47.220
are gonna determine whether it's a hard shadow

234
00:09:47.759 --> 00:09:48.600
or a soft shadow.

235
00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:51.580
What that means is as I scale this light up here

236
00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:53.740
in the details, not in the scene,

237
00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:55.139
it's easy to get lost with that

238
00:09:55.139 --> 00:09:57.340
and think it's like 3D Studio Max or Maya

239
00:09:57.340 --> 00:09:59.840
but in the details, if I mess with this,

240
00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:01.500
the light will actually lose energy.

241
00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:04.659
See it gets softer as I increase that size.

242
00:10:04.659 --> 00:10:07.179
So that's one way to understand

243
00:10:07.179 --> 00:10:10.340
how to keep those shadows stronger

244
00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:14.539
or have them softer is messing with the actual scale

245
00:10:14.539 --> 00:10:17.120
or I should say height in this case

246
00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:21.200
and width to be able to control that.

247
00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:22.039
There we go.

248
00:10:22.039 --> 00:10:23.039
We can soften it up if you want to.

249
00:10:23.039 --> 00:10:24.740
I'm gonna keep it right about here.

250
00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.440
I think that works pretty well.

251
00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:28.879
And then from here, we can add a few more lights

252
00:10:28.879 --> 00:10:31.600
whether they're coming in from the outdoors or not.

253
00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:34.559
And we can also mess with a little bit of our exposure.

254
00:10:34.559 --> 00:10:37.080
If we want it to be a little bit more moody

255
00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:41.159
we can darken it just a bit there if we wanted to.

256
00:10:41.159 --> 00:10:44.480
And from here we can add in some external lights

257
00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:47.720
for our computers so I can add in another Rec Light.

258
00:10:48.559 --> 00:10:51.399
And again, point to note Rec Lights and Point Lights

259
00:10:51.399 --> 00:10:53.740
are two of the most expensive lights to work with.

260
00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:56.600
The Rec Light is like a series of point lights

261
00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:59.840
set into a square initially

262
00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:01.799
and can be shaped into whatever you want

263
00:11:01.799 --> 00:11:03.159
such as a rectangle.

